---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/lc/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/lc/Footer.astro';
import Container from '../../components/lc/Container.astro';
import ArticleHeader from '../../components/lc/ArticleHeader.astro';
import Stats from '../../components/lc/Stats.astro';
import Prose from '../../components/lc/Prose.astro';
import Label from '../../components/lc/Label.astro';
import CtaPanel from '../../components/lc/CtaPanel.astro';
import Button from '../../components/lc/Button.astro';
import Screenshot from '../../components/Screenshot.astro';
import { facts } from '../../data/facts';

// Every number, title, name and date on this page comes from src/data/facts.ts.
// Do not retype one here - npm run check:facts will fail the build.
---

<Layout
  title="Health Office App"
  description={`A Firebase + React student check-in system with automated, same-day notifications to teachers and secretaries, launched school-wide for ${facts.scale.students} students at ${facts.aisc.schoolShort} Chennai.`}
>
  <Nav />
  <main>

    <ArticleHeader
      back={{ href: '/work', label: 'Back to all work' }}
      label="Full-stack · Firebase + React"
      sublabel="Chapter 3 · Go school-wide, and go fast"
      title="Health Office App"
    >
      A student health check-in system that closes the communication loop the moment a student walks
      into the nurse's office, automatically letting the right teacher and secretary know, in real time.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <Container>
      <article class="flex max-w-3xl flex-col gap-lc-8 pt-lc-8">
        <Stats
          items={[
          { value: facts.scale.students, label: 'Students covered' },
          { value: 'School-wide', label: 'Launch · Jun 3, 2026' },
          { value: 'Same-day', label: 'Notifications live' },
          { value: '2', label: 'Audiences notified' },
          ]}
        />

        <Prose>

          <Label>Overview</Label>
          <h2>What it is</h2>
            <p>
              The Health Office App is a Firebase + React web application for student check-ins at the
              school health office. When a student checks in, the system automatically notifies the
              relevant teachers and secretaries, so the people responsible for that student always know
              where they are and why.
            </p>
            <p>
              It launched school-wide on <strong>June 3, 2026</strong>, covering the
              full student body of {facts.scale.students}, with automated notifications going live the same week on June 9.
            </p>

          <Label>The Problem</Label>
          <h2>A communication gap with real stakes</h2>
            <p>
              When a student leaves class for the health office, a gap opens. The teacher may not know
              where the student went or whether they're coming back. The front office may not have a record.
              Paper logs and verbal hand-offs don't scale across a 700-student school, and in a health
              context, knowing a student's whereabouts in real time isn't a nice-to-have, it's a duty of care.
            </p>

        <Screenshot
          src="/images/work/health-office-checkin.png"
          alt="The Health Office check-in screen used by the school nurse"
          caption="The check-in screen. Built in Firebase and React, launched school-wide in six days."
        />

          <Label>What I Built</Label>
          <h2>Check-in plus automatic notification</h2>
            <p>
              I built a full-stack web app on Firebase and React. The front end gives health-office staff
              a fast, simple check-in flow; Firebase handles the data and the real-time logic behind it:
            </p>
          <ul>
            {[
              ['Student check-in', 'A quick, reliable way for health-office staff to log a student arriving.'],
              ['Automated teacher notifications', 'The relevant teacher is notified automatically, so they know exactly where their student is.'],
              ['Automated secretary notifications', 'Front-office staff get the same real-time visibility, closing the records gap.'],
              ['Real-time backend', 'Firebase keeps the data and notifications consistent and instant across the school.'],
            ].map(([title, desc]) => (
              <li><strong>{title}.</strong> {desc}</li>
            ))}
          </ul>

        <Screenshot
          src="/images/work/health-office-notification.png"
          alt="An automated same-day notification email sent to a teacher"
          caption="The automated same-day notification, the part that actually closed the duty-of-care gap."
        />

          <Label>Impact &amp; Results</Label>
          <h2>From paper trail to real-time visibility</h2>
          <Stats
            items={[
              { value: facts.scale.students, label: 'Students covered', note: 'entire student body' },
              { value: 'Jun 3', label: 'School-wide launch', note: '2026' },
              { value: 'Jun 9', label: 'Notifications live', note: 'same week' },
            ]}
          />
            <p>
              The app gave the school real-time visibility into student health check-ins across the
              entire student body, and replaced informal hand-offs with automatic, reliable notifications
              to the people who need them. From launch to live notifications took less than a week, a fast path from shipped to fully operational.
            </p>

          <Label>Lessons Learned</Label>
          <h2>What I'd carry forward</h2>
          <ul>
            {[
              ['Pick the stack that ships.', 'Firebase + React let one person build, launch, and wire up real-time notifications for a whole school in a tight window, the right tools collapse the distance between idea and live.'],
              ['Close the loop, don’t just log it.', 'A check-in record is useful; an automatic notification to the right teacher is what actually changes behavior and reduces worry.'],
              ['In a duty-of-care context, speed matters.', 'Going from launch to live notifications in the same week meant the safety benefit landed immediately, not next term.'],
            ].map(([title, desc]) => (
              <li><strong set:html={title} /> <span set:html={desc} /></li>
            ))}
          </ul>

        </Prose>
      </article>
    </Container>

    <CtaPanel title="Need a school tool built and shipped fast?">
      <Fragment slot="actions">
        <Button href="/work-with-me#contact">Let's Talk</Button>
        <Button href="/work" variant="secondary" tone="dark">More Case Studies</Button>
      </Fragment>
    </CtaPanel>

  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
